\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Three failures of normalization}
\date{2026-10-25}

\p{The [normalization theorem](fdip-002T) distinguishes infinite mass from
zero mass. In positive dimension, zero precision has #{S=0} and weight
one, so its integral is the infinite [Euclidean volume](fdip-002M).
Invertibility alone is insufficient: on #{\mathbb R^2},
#{Q=\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)} has weight #{e^{-x^2/2+y^2/2}}.
On #{|x|\leq1} the first factor is at least #{e^{-1/2}}, while the
integral in #{y} diverges. Neither weight defines the normalized
[Gaussian law](fdip-0005) on that full space.}
\p{For a general nonnegative weight #{w\equiv0}, the integral is zero,
so division cannot normalize it. This differs from zero precision.
On #{E=0}, the unique endomorphism is invertible and vacuously positive,
its Gaussian weight is one, and its normalizer is one. A singular law
on a proper subspace requires that subspace's own reference measure;
inserting a pseudoinverse does not normalize a divergent ambient density.}
\p{These are direct calculations from the spectral and scalar ingredients
of \ref{fdip-002T}; the scalar positive and nonpositive distinction uses
\citet{Section 2, PDF pp. 1-2}{conradGaussianIntegral} with the
explicit bounds proved in \ref{fdip-002S}.}
